<h1>TLinkButton</h1>
<com:DocLink ClassPath="Prado.Web.UI.WebControls.TLinkButton" />

<p class="block-content">
<tt>TLinkButton</tt> matches <a href="?page=Controls.Button">TButton</a> in behavior and
differs in appearance. It renders an <tt>&lt;a&gt;</tt> element, so it looks like a link
and acts like a button. <tt>Text</tt> holds the link text. A button with an empty
<tt>Text</tt> renders its body content instead, so a template nests an image or styled
markup inside the tag.
</p>

<com:TTextHighlighter Language="prado" CssClass="source block-content">
&lt;com:TLinkButton Text="Delete" OnClick="deleteRow" /&gt;
&lt;com:TLinkButton OnClick="deleteRow"&gt;
  &lt;com:TImage ImageUrl="trash.png" AlternateText="" /&gt; Delete
&lt;/com:TLinkButton&gt;
</com:TTextHighlighter>

<h2>Events and commands</h2>

<p class="block-content">
The control raises <tt>OnClick</tt> and <tt>OnCommand</tt>, as a button does.
<tt>OnCommand</tt> bubbles to the ancestor controls, and carries <tt>CommandName</tt> and
<tt>CommandParameter</tt> in its event parameter. That is how a repeater or a data grid
tells apart the link buttons its items render. Clicking triggers validation when
<tt>CausesValidation</tt> is true, restricted to a <tt>ValidationGroup</tt>.
</p>

<h2>The control needs client script</h2>

<p class="block-content">
A link has no way of its own to post a form, so the control writes an <tt>href</tt> that
calls into the PRADO client script. Setting <tt>EnableClientScript</tt> to false leaves it
with no way to submit, and the control then renders itself as <tt>disabled</tt>. A page
that must work without JavaScript uses <a href="?page=Controls.Button">TButton</a>
instead.
</p>

<p class="block-content">
<tt>IsDefaultButton</tt> makes the button respond to the return key inside the panel that
holds it.
</p>
